<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Trinidad and Tobago</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Republic of Trinidad and Tobago</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinidad">Trinidad</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tobago">Tobago</a> is the southernmost archipelagic country in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a>, lying off the coast of Venezuela. Indigenous peoples settled Trinidad long before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Christopher Columbus</a> reached it in 1498, and its oldest known settlement, Banwari Trace, dates to about 5000 BCE. Spanish governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-maria-chacon">José María Chacón</a> surrendered Trinidad to a British fleet in 1797, and Trinidad and Tobago were ceded to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> in 1802 as separate states. They were unified in 1889, became independent in 1962 and a republic in 1976. Indian indentured workers came to its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> plantations in the 19th century.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Trinidad and Tobago</b> gained independence from Britain, with Eric Williams as prime minister.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-31">31 August in history</a></li></ul></section>
